# PeckNSend

PeckNSend is a local multiplayer Unity game prototype where players control birds, collect mail, and deliver it to matching mailboxes for points.

## My Contributions

The following folders contain the systems and scripts I worked on:

* `FSM/`
* `Models/`
* `Presenters/`
* `Debugging/`
* `Singleton/`

I did **not** create the code in:

* `Background/`
* `Mail/`
* `MailboxLogic/`
* `Player/`
* `MailShooter.cs`

## Architecture & Design Decisions

The main goal of the project was to create a fun and functional game within the available development time, rather than to build an extensive production architecture.
However, I tried to use established programming patterns to keep systems understandable and maintainable.

I implemented much of the application's overall flow using **finite state machines** and a **MVP structure**.

The main systems I worked on include:

* Scene flow between boot, main menu, player join, play, and result scenes
* Main menu screen state management
* Player joining and ready-up tracking
* Persistent player and game-session data
* Match statistics and score reporting
* Result screen presentation
* Direct-entry scene bootstrappers for faster Unity playtesting

## Notable Systems

### Scene Flow System

* `Presenters/SceneSwitchSystem/SceneManagerPresenter.cs`
* `Models/SceneSwithSystem/SceneManagerModel.cs`
* `Models/SceneSwithSystem/SceneManagerFSM.cs`
* `Models/SceneSwithSystem/SceneBaseState.cs`

This system controls the main flow between boot, main menu, player join, play, and result scenes. 
I used a finite state machine so scene transitions are requested through clear state-specific methods instead of being triggered directly from scattered UI scripts.

### Persistent Game Session System

* `Presenters/GameSessionSystem/GameSessionPresenter.cs`
* `Models/GameSessionSystem/GameSessionModel.cs`
* `Models/PlayerJoinScene/JoinedPlayerData.cs`
* `Models/PlayScene/MatchStatsModel.cs`
* `Models/PlayScene/PlayerMatchStatsModel.cs`

This system stores player and match data across scene changes. 
It keeps track of joined players, controller/device information, bird selections, and delivered mail scores so gameplay and result scenes can share the same session data.

### Direct Scene Testing Tools

* `Debugging/MainMenuSceneDirectEntryBootstrapper.cs`
* `Debugging/PlayerJoinSceneDirectEntryBootstrapper.cs`
* `Debugging/PlaySceneDirectEntryBootstrapper.cs`
* `Debugging/ResultSceneDirectEntryBootstrapper.cs`

These bootstrappers make individual Unity scenes easier to test by creating required persistent services and debug session data when entering a scene directly.

## Current Status

This repository is intended as a **portfolio and code sample** from a Unity game project rather than a polished production release.
The game itself was developed under project deadlines, and some features were still unfinished when development ended, including pause handling.
The repository therefore reflects both completed systems and areas I would improve if development continued.

## What I Learned

This project gave me practical experience with:

* Structuring Unity projects beyond single-purpose `MonoBehaviour` scripts
* Separating application state from UI presentation
* Using finite state machines to manage application and menu flow
* Managing persistent data across Unity scenes
* Supporting multiplayer player joining and ready-up workflows
* Designing systems that need to communicate across multiple scenes
* Building development tools to reduce iteration time
* Working within an existing codebase shared by multiple programmers

It also gave me more experience deciding **when additional architecture is useful and when a simpler solution is sufficient**.

## Future Improvements

If I continued development, I would focus on:

* Finishing incomplete behaviour, such as pause handling
* Improving null checks and validation so missing dependencies are easier to diagnose
* Refactoring older folder names, class names, and comments for consistency
* Removing remaining development `TODO` comments
* Adding automated tests for state transitions, ready-up logic, and scoring
* Documenting scene setup and required Unity packages (how to use)
* Improving scene transitions with asynchronous scene loading and a persistent transition service
